1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,580
This is Doing and Feeling, the long version. Episode 1, Two Questions. Before we begin, a note on what you're listening to. This series was written by Claude, an AI model made by Anthropic, and both voices you'll hear are synthetic. I'm the narrator.

2
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:21,240
The second voice belongs to a sparring partner with a background in machine learning,

3
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:26,840
whose job is to push back with the strongest objection or the sharpest question when one is due.

4
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:31,240
The series is about two questions that are constantly confused with each other.

5
00:00:31,520 --> 00:00:33,380
The first is about intelligence.

6
00:00:33,740 --> 00:00:38,500
What it is, how it is measured, how it evolved, how brains achieve it,

7
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:41,060
and how machines now achieve a great deal of it.

8
00:00:41,220 --> 00:00:42,860
The second is about consciousness.

9
00:00:43,370 --> 00:00:46,000
why some physical systems seem to have an inner life,

10
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:47,700
something it is like to be them,

11
00:00:47,860 --> 00:00:50,920
and whether we can explain that, detect it, or build it.

12
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:54,720
The neuroscientist Anil Seth puts the distinction simply.

13
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:56,780
Intelligence is about doing.

14
00:00:57,440 --> 00:00:59,300
Consciousness is about being and feeling.

15
00:00:59,540 --> 00:01:00,880
The two could come apart.

16
00:01:01,160 --> 00:01:03,600
A system might be extraordinarily good at doing,

17
00:01:03,820 --> 00:01:05,060
with nothing felt at all.

18
00:01:05,420 --> 00:01:07,720
Something else might feel without being very clever,

19
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:10,120
a newborn or a fish in pain.

20
00:01:10,580 --> 00:01:13,680
Whether they really do come apart, in animals and in machines,

21
00:01:13,980 --> 00:01:16,980
is the question running underneath all 14 episodes.

22
00:01:17,320 --> 00:01:18,960
And it has stopped being academic.

23
00:01:19,580 --> 00:01:21,780
Machines now do much of what minds do,

24
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:24,780
and people are already asking whether any of them feel anything.

25
00:01:25,140 --> 00:01:26,140
Here's the route.

26
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:29,040
Part 1, 6 episodes, is about doing.

27
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:32,060
Human intelligence and how we learn to measure it.

28
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:33,720
Minds in other bodies.

29
00:01:33,960 --> 00:01:36,000
Intelligence as a mathematical idea.

30
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:37,560
Life as computation.

31
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:39,840
The brain as a prediction machine.

32
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:45,500
and the machines themselves. Part two, five episodes, is about feeling. The hard problem,

33
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:49,580
the case that consciousness is a kind of illusion, the full map of positions,

34
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:55,800
the experimental evidence, and the scientific theories on trial. Part three, three episodes,

35
00:01:56,080 --> 00:02:01,260
brings the two questions together. What we can actually check in today's AI, the case that it

36
00:02:01,260 --> 00:02:06,400
is not conscious, and what we should do while we do not know. That middle episode of part three

37
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:12,100
is deliberate. An AI narrating whether AI might be conscious has a stake in the answer.

38
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:17,720
So the case against gets an episode of its own, argued as strongly as I can argue it.

39
00:02:18,100 --> 00:02:22,480
Fair enough. But why open a series about minds with IQ tests?

40
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:27,380
Because the modern concept of intelligence was shaped by the attempt to measure it,

41
00:02:27,540 --> 00:02:32,160
and so was a great deal of harm. Here is the argument of this episode.

42
00:02:32,580 --> 00:02:38,660
The general factor of intelligence is one of the best replicated and most predictive findings in psychology.

43
00:02:38,740 --> 00:02:48,260
But what causes it, what its heritability means, and how the field's history shaped it are all far less settled than either its fans or its critics tend to claim.

44
00:02:48,540 --> 00:02:57,820
If you want to reason clearly about intelligence in animals and machines, you need to know what this concept was built to do, what it gets right, and where it stops.

45
00:02:58,780 --> 00:03:03,340
The story usually starts with Francis Galton, Charles Darwin's half-cousin.

46
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:07,260
In the 1880s, Galton ran a laboratory in London

47
00:03:07,260 --> 00:03:10,280
where visitors paid a small fee to have their grip strength,

48
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:13,620
eyesight, hearing, and other simple capacities measured.

49
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:18,200
He believed ability was inherited and could be read off such simple measures.

50
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:22,800
He was largely wrong about the measures, and he also coined the word eugenics.

51
00:03:22,980 --> 00:03:25,540
The first useful test came from France.

52
00:03:26,010 --> 00:03:32,580
In 1904, the French Education Ministry set up a commission on children who needed extra help at school.

53
00:03:32,980 --> 00:03:39,460
The psychologist Alfred Binet, one of its members, set out to find an objective way to identify them.

54
00:03:39,660 --> 00:03:44,780
With Theodore Simon, he built a scale of reasoning, memory, and comprehension problems,

55
00:03:44,920 --> 00:03:49,360
graded by age, so that a child's performance could be compared with what was typical.

56
00:03:49,980 --> 00:03:52,220
Binet was cautious about what he had made.

57
00:03:52,220 --> 00:03:57,160
He did not believe intelligence was a single fixed quantity, and he warned against the

58
00:03:57,160 --> 00:03:59,420
pessimism of labeling a child for life.

59
00:03:59,580 --> 00:04:04,560
He meant it as a tool for finding children who needed help, not for ranking people for

60
00:04:04,560 --> 00:04:04,920
life.

61
00:04:05,160 --> 00:04:08,100
That caution did not survive the crossing to America.

62
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:14,780
In 1916, Lewis Terman at Stanford revised the test and popularized the intelligence

63
00:04:14,780 --> 00:04:15,340
quotient.

64
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:21,420
The German psychologist William Stern had proposed dividing mental age by actual age,

65
00:04:22,220 --> 00:04:27,860
multiplied the result by 100. During the First World War, the United States Army gave group

66
00:04:27,860 --> 00:04:35,380
tests to about 1.75 million recruits. Intelligence tests were also used to label people feeble-minded

67
00:04:35,380 --> 00:04:41,340
under state laws allowing compulsory sterilization, the first of which was passed in 1907.

68
00:04:41,740 --> 00:04:48,620
In 1927, in Buck v. Bell, the Supreme Court of the United States upheld Virginia's law.

69
00:04:48,620 --> 00:04:51,380
One part of the standard story is disputed.

70
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,740
Critics such as Leon Kamen and Stephen Jay Gould

71
00:04:54,740 --> 00:05:00,060
argued that the Army data helped push through the 1924 law-restricting immigration.

72
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:05,760
In 1983, Mark Snyderman and Richard Herenstein replied that many testers

73
00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:07,700
were themselves skeptical of those data,

74
00:05:07,900 --> 00:05:11,960
and that Congress barely mentioned testing in its hearings and debates.

75
00:05:12,340 --> 00:05:15,900
Historians still argue about how much influence testing had.

76
00:05:16,620 --> 00:05:21,360
Beyond dispute is that the eugenics movement used the language of measured intelligence

77
00:05:21,360 --> 00:05:25,360
and that real people were sterilized under the laws it inspired.

78
00:05:25,760 --> 00:05:30,100
So the concept has baggage. Is there anything solid underneath it?

79
00:05:30,420 --> 00:05:34,640
Quite a lot. And the solid part is worth understanding precisely,

80
00:05:34,640 --> 00:05:38,200
because both sides of the public argument tend to get it wrong.

81
00:05:39,280 --> 00:05:46,020
Also in 1904, the English psychologist Charles Spearman analyzed children's results across

82
00:05:46,020 --> 00:05:51,160
school subjects and simple sensory tasks, including telling musical pitches apart.

83
00:05:51,560 --> 00:05:54,620
Every measure correlated positively with every other.

84
00:05:54,920 --> 00:05:58,160
Children who did well at one thing tended to do well at others.

85
00:05:58,680 --> 00:06:04,120
Spearman proposed a general factor behind those correlations, later called G, plus a

86
00:06:04,120 --> 00:06:05,860
specific factor for each test.

87
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:09,340
The pattern itself, now called the positive manifold,

88
00:06:09,900 --> 00:06:13,060
has been replicated about as well as anything in psychology.

89
00:06:13,400 --> 00:06:14,740
The picture got richer.

90
00:06:15,020 --> 00:06:17,720
In the 1930s, Louis Thurstone argued

91
00:06:17,720 --> 00:06:19,500
for several primary mental abilities,

92
00:06:19,800 --> 00:06:22,340
verbal, spatial, numerical, and others.

93
00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:25,400
But his primary abilities correlated with one another,

94
00:06:25,600 --> 00:06:28,100
so a general factor reappeared one level up.

95
00:06:28,580 --> 00:06:30,420
Raymond Cattell and John Horne

96
00:06:30,420 --> 00:06:32,040
distinguished fluid intelligence,

97
00:06:32,420 --> 00:06:34,200
reasoning about novel problems

98
00:06:36,140 --> 00:06:42,800
accumulated knowledge and skill. In 1993, John Carroll reanalysed more than 400 data sets and

99
00:06:42,800 --> 00:06:49,320
proposed three levels, dozens of narrow abilities, eight broad ones, and G at the top. Merged with

100
00:06:49,320 --> 00:06:54,240
Cattle and Horn's work, it became the Cattle-Horn-Carroll model, with about 10 broad abilities.

101
00:06:54,740 --> 00:06:59,840
Most major test batteries are now built on it, and so, as we'll see in episode three,

102
00:07:00,120 --> 00:07:03,780
is one prominent attempt to define artificial general intelligence.

103
00:07:05,300 --> 00:07:09,180
Statistically, G is the first factor of a correlation matrix.

104
00:07:09,320 --> 00:07:12,020
Does it correspond to anything in the head?

105
00:07:12,320 --> 00:07:16,420
That's the right question, and the statistics alone cannot answer it.

106
00:07:16,620 --> 00:07:20,580
There are at least four rival explanations of why abilities correlate.

107
00:07:20,880 --> 00:07:24,300
The first is Spearman's, a single common cause,

108
00:07:24,560 --> 00:07:26,720
something like general processing efficiency,

109
00:07:27,100 --> 00:07:30,600
perhaps neural speed or the integrity of the brain's wiring.

110
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:35,240
The second is Godfrey Thompson's sampling theory, from 1916.

111
00:07:35,660 --> 00:07:38,860
Every test draws on many small mental processes,

112
00:07:39,160 --> 00:07:43,680
and two tests correlate to the extent that they happen to sample overlapping ones.

113
00:07:44,180 --> 00:07:46,440
No single general ability is needed.

114
00:07:46,720 --> 00:07:52,320
The third is mutualism, proposed by Han Vander Maas and colleagues in 2006.

115
00:07:52,780 --> 00:07:57,420
Abilities start out fairly independent and reinforce each other during development.

116
00:07:58,000 --> 00:07:59,560
Vocabulary helps reading.

117
00:08:00,600 --> 00:08:06,440
reasoning, reasoning helps you learn more words. Over the years, that mutual boosting produces

118
00:08:06,440 --> 00:08:12,640
positive correlations everywhere. The fourth is process overlap theory, from Christoph Kovach

119
00:08:12,640 --> 00:08:19,380
and Andrew Conway in 2016. Executive processes such as working memory and attentional control

120
00:08:19,380 --> 00:08:25,940
are involved in a large share of test items, while more specific processes are involved in only some.

121
00:08:25,940 --> 00:08:30,460
because weak executive control drags performance down across the board,

122
00:08:30,700 --> 00:08:33,100
that shared load produces the general factor.

123
00:08:33,420 --> 00:08:34,660
Here's the deep point.

124
00:08:34,880 --> 00:08:38,700
All four can produce essentially the same pattern of correlations,

125
00:08:38,920 --> 00:08:42,080
so correlations alone cannot pick out the mechanism.

126
00:08:42,580 --> 00:08:47,700
Researchers now try to tell the explanations apart with data on development and change.

127
00:08:47,960 --> 00:08:53,700
The psychometrician Denny Borsboom and his colleagues sharpened a related point in 2003.

128
00:08:54,320 --> 00:09:00,780
Treating a factor as a real cause of test scores is a substantive hypothesis that needs evidence of its own.

129
00:09:00,980 --> 00:09:07,400
And even then, it describes differences between people, not the workings inside any one person's head.

130
00:09:07,660 --> 00:09:09,820
The same rigor applies to comparisons.

131
00:09:10,240 --> 00:09:15,740
If you want to say that two groups or two generations differ in the same underlying ability,

132
00:09:15,980 --> 00:09:19,540
you first have to show that the test measures the same thing in both.

133
00:09:19,860 --> 00:09:22,480
That property is called measurement invariance.

134
00:09:23,740 --> 00:09:28,840
It comes back twice before this episode is over, and again when we get to machines.

135
00:09:29,200 --> 00:09:32,440
There's an AI version of the common cause story.

136
00:09:32,880 --> 00:09:39,060
Benchmark scores across language models correlate to, and the obvious common cause is scale.

137
00:09:39,520 --> 00:09:42,500
More compute makes a model better at nearly everything.

138
00:09:42,960 --> 00:09:48,320
Researchers have run exactly that analysis, with a twist we'll get to in episode 3.

139
00:09:49,280 --> 00:09:50,880
Does G predict anything?

140
00:09:51,440 --> 00:09:54,460
Yes, though less dramatically than its fans often say.

141
00:09:54,900 --> 00:09:58,080
Test scores correlate around 0.5 with school grades.

142
00:09:58,420 --> 00:10:03,600
For job performance, a famous 1998 synthesis by Frank Schmidt and John Hunter

143
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:06,100
put the correlation at about 0.5,

144
00:10:06,300 --> 00:10:11,760
which made cognitive ability look like the best single predictor of how well people do their jobs.

145
00:10:11,940 --> 00:10:19,760
In 2022, Paul Sackett and colleagues argued that the statistical corrections behind that estimate were too aggressive,

146
00:10:20,880 --> 00:10:26,940
it to about 0.3, which put cognitive ability behind structured interviews and several other

147
00:10:26,940 --> 00:10:34,500
methods. In 2024, Sackett's group analyzed more than 150 samples collected since the year 2000

148
00:10:34,500 --> 00:10:42,220
and found a correlation of 0.16, or about 0.22 after correction. Other researchers dispute those

149
00:10:42,220 --> 00:10:47,520
corrections, so the right number is genuinely contested. But the direction of travel in the

150
00:10:47,520 --> 00:10:53,420
estimates has been down. In the field called cognitive epidemiology, higher test scores in

151
00:10:53,420 --> 00:11:00,420
youth go with longer lives. A 2011 meta-analysis by Catherine Calvin and colleagues, covering about

152
00:11:00,420 --> 00:11:07,000
1.1 million people, found roughly a quarter lower risk of death for each standard deviation higher

153
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:12,360
score in youth. In the studies that could check, adjusting for adult social position shrank that

154
00:11:12,360 --> 00:11:18,400
association by about a third and adjusting for education by about half. That suggests the link

155
00:11:18,400 --> 00:11:25,260
runs partly through or is shared with schooling and circumstance. So G predicts real outcomes.

156
00:11:25,520 --> 00:11:31,400
It does so as a statistical tendency across large populations, not as anyone's destiny.

157
00:11:32,520 --> 00:11:38,840
Now heritability, one of the most misunderstood numbers in science. Heritability is the share of

158
00:11:38,840 --> 00:11:44,360
the variation in a trait within a particular population and a particular range of environments

159
00:11:44,360 --> 00:11:50,540
that goes with genetic variation. It is a property of a population, not of a person.

160
00:11:50,780 --> 00:11:55,200
You cannot say that some percentage of your own intelligence comes from your genes.

161
00:11:55,640 --> 00:12:00,860
Heritability can change when environments change, and it says nothing about whether a trait can be

162
00:12:00,860 --> 00:12:07,640
altered. Phenylketonuria is a single gene condition that, untreated, causes severe intellectual

163
00:12:08,840 --> 00:12:12,840
A special diet, started early, prevents almost all of the damage,

164
00:12:13,220 --> 00:12:15,860
highly heritable and largely preventable.

165
00:12:16,200 --> 00:12:17,840
With those caveats in place,

166
00:12:18,220 --> 00:12:22,420
twin and adoption studies show a striking pattern called the Wilson effect.

167
00:12:22,800 --> 00:12:25,920
The heritability of test scores rises with age,

168
00:12:26,140 --> 00:12:30,740
from roughly 20% in infancy to around 40% by age 9

169
00:12:30,740 --> 00:12:34,560
and to something like 70-80% in early adulthood

170
00:12:34,560 --> 00:12:38,180
in the mostly high-income Western samples that have been studied.

171
00:12:38,840 --> 00:12:44,640
what that number means. In those populations, most of the differences between adults go along

172
00:12:44,640 --> 00:12:49,800
with genetic differences. It does not mean that most of any one person's intelligence comes from

173
00:12:49,800 --> 00:12:55,420
their genes. One explanation is that as people grow up, they increasingly choose their own

174
00:12:55,420 --> 00:13:01,080
environments, books, friends, and jobs in ways that amplify their starting dispositions.

175
00:13:01,560 --> 00:13:08,220
Heritability also depends on circumstances. In 2003, Eric Turkheimer and colleagues reported

176
00:13:08,840 --> 00:13:13,900
poor American families, genes accounted for almost none of the variation in children's scores,

177
00:13:14,200 --> 00:13:20,500
while among affluent families they accounted for most of it. A 2016 meta-analysis by Elliot

178
00:13:20,500 --> 00:13:25,800
Tucker-Draub and Timothy Bates found that pattern in the United States, but not in Western Europe

179
00:13:25,800 --> 00:13:31,360
or Australia. One reading is that deprivation suppresses differences that would otherwise show,

180
00:13:31,520 --> 00:13:36,460
and that how strongly it does so depends on how unequal a society's environments are.

181
00:13:36,460 --> 00:13:45,660
But a large later study of Florida twins and siblings found no such pattern in school test scores, so even the American result is still debated.

182
00:13:46,140 --> 00:13:48,260
Molecular genetics added a twist.

183
00:13:48,780 --> 00:13:53,920
Genome-wide studies find thousands of genetic variants, each with a tiny effect.

184
00:13:54,620 --> 00:14:00,460
Combined into polygenic scores, the largest study so far explains about 12 to 16 percent

185
00:14:00,460 --> 00:14:05,460
of the differences in how much schooling people complete, well below twin study estimates

186
00:14:05,460 --> 00:14:06,680
for the same outcome.

187
00:14:06,860 --> 00:14:11,940
And when you compare siblings within the same family, the score's apparent effect shrinks

188
00:14:11,940 --> 00:14:12,700
by about half.

189
00:14:13,100 --> 00:14:18,020
That suggests part of what the score's capture comes from family background, such as the

190
00:14:18,020 --> 00:14:22,980
environment parents create and who partners with whom, rather than from a person's own

191
00:14:23,920 --> 00:14:31,740
directly. Meanwhile, the environment demonstrably moves scores. A 2018 meta-analysis by Stuart Ritchie

192
00:14:31,740 --> 00:14:37,180
and Tucker Draub, drawing on natural experiments such as changes in the school-leaving age,

193
00:14:37,660 --> 00:14:43,060
estimated that each extra year of education raises IQ by roughly 1 to 5 points.

194
00:14:43,340 --> 00:14:49,900
High heritability and big environmental effects at the same time. That sounds like a contradiction.

195
00:14:49,900 --> 00:14:53,300
It is not one, and the next finding shows why.

196
00:14:54,180 --> 00:14:59,180
The political scientist James Flynn documented that average IQ scores

197
00:14:59,180 --> 00:15:04,780
rose by roughly three points per decade through much of the 20th century, in many countries.

198
00:15:05,320 --> 00:15:09,800
The gains were largest on abstract reasoning tests, like Raven's matrices,

199
00:15:10,220 --> 00:15:12,800
and smallest on vocabulary and arithmetic.

200
00:15:13,120 --> 00:15:15,900
That is far too fast for genetic change.

201
00:15:16,180 --> 00:15:18,900
Candidate causes include better nutrition and health,

202
00:15:19,980 --> 00:15:25,800
smaller families, and what Flynn called scientific spectacles, a cultural habit of

203
00:15:25,800 --> 00:15:31,600
classifying and reasoning abstractly. He liked to cite interviews from the 1930s in which the

204
00:15:31,600 --> 00:15:37,340
psychologist Alexander Luria posed simple syllogisms to unschooled farmers in Central Asia,

205
00:15:37,500 --> 00:15:41,600
who often declined to reason about premises outside their own experience.

206
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:48,740
Here, measurement invariance comes back. In 2004, Jelt, Wickerts, and colleagues tested five data

207
00:15:49,920 --> 00:15:55,860
cohorts 10 to 60 years apart, and found that invariants failed in all of them. The tests were

208
00:15:55,860 --> 00:16:01,460
not measuring quite the same thing in earlier and later cohorts, so the gains cannot simply be read

209
00:16:01,460 --> 00:16:07,840
as gains in the underlying ability. And in some countries, the rise has reversed. Bernd Bratsberg

210
00:16:07,840 --> 00:16:15,480
and Ole Roo-Jayborg analyzed the scores of about 730,000 Norwegian conscripts. Scores rose for men

211
00:16:15,480 --> 00:16:21,840
born up to the mid-1970s and then fell. Crucially, the decline shows up between brothers in the same

212
00:16:21,840 --> 00:16:27,360
family, which rules out explanations based on which families have more children and points to

213
00:16:27,360 --> 00:16:33,480
the environment. That resolves the apparent paradox. Heritability describes variation within

214
00:16:33,480 --> 00:16:39,540
a population at one time. The Flynn effect is a shift of the whole population across time.

215
00:16:39,720 --> 00:16:45,100
A trait can be highly heritable and still move a lot when everyone's environment changes.

216
00:16:46,220 --> 00:16:50,280
That logic carries straight into the most contentious topic in this field,

217
00:16:50,500 --> 00:16:52,360
average differences between groups.

218
00:16:52,720 --> 00:16:57,040
The key principle is that heritability within groups does not, by itself,

219
00:16:57,480 --> 00:16:59,600
tell you the causes of differences between groups.

220
00:16:59,980 --> 00:17:03,860
The geneticist Richard Luontin's illustration is still the clearest.

221
00:17:04,220 --> 00:17:10,480
Take a bag of genetically varied seed and plant one handful in rich soil and another in poor soil.

222
00:17:10,640 --> 00:17:14,280
Within each field, differences in height may be largely genetic.

223
00:17:15,480 --> 00:17:20,040
both handfuls came from the same bag, the difference between the fields is entirely

224
00:17:20,040 --> 00:17:25,520
environmental. The analogy shows that high heritability within groups is compatible

225
00:17:25,520 --> 00:17:31,560
with a wholly environmental gap between them. By itself, it cannot tell you which situation

226
00:17:31,560 --> 00:17:37,260
holds for any real human groups. On the evidence itself, the record runs like this.

227
00:17:37,500 --> 00:17:44,400
After the bell curve reignited public argument in 1994, the American Psychological Association

228
00:17:45,480 --> 00:17:52,540
task force. Its 1996 report concluded that test scores predict important outcomes. It found that

229
00:17:52,540 --> 00:17:57,160
the average difference between black and white Americans, then about one standard deviation,

230
00:17:57,480 --> 00:18:02,480
did not come from obvious bias in the tests and did not simply reflect social class.

231
00:18:03,280 --> 00:18:08,940
Explanations based on culture and social position might be right, it said, but had little direct

232
00:18:08,940 --> 00:18:15,100
support, and there was no direct support at all for a genetic explanation. Its bottom line was

233
00:18:15,480 --> 00:18:21,860
one knew the cause. Here measurement invariance matters again. Unlike the gains across generations,

234
00:18:22,480 --> 00:18:27,240
studies comparing black and white Americans have mostly found that the tests measure the

235
00:18:27,240 --> 00:18:32,140
same abilities in both groups. So the gap is not simply an artifact of the tests.

236
00:18:32,380 --> 00:18:39,720
That tells you what the gap is, not what causes it. In 2012, a review by Richard Nisbet, Flynn,

237
00:18:40,260 --> 00:18:44,780
Turkheimer, and others reported that the gap had narrowed by about a third of a standard deviation

238
00:18:45,480 --> 00:18:50,580
decades, and its authors argued that group differences in IQ are best understood as

239
00:18:50,580 --> 00:18:57,260
environmental in origin. Others, such as Charles Murray, accepted some narrowing over the century,

240
00:18:57,460 --> 00:19:03,580
but argued that it had stalled by the late 1980s. And some researchers, most prominently Arthur

241
00:19:03,580 --> 00:19:09,160
Jensen and Philippe Rushton, argued that genes explain part of the gap, pointing to indirect

242
00:19:09,160 --> 00:19:14,860
evidence such as heritability and adoption studies. Critics reply that indirect evidence

243
00:19:15,480 --> 00:19:22,040
cannot separate genetic from environmental causes. Opinion among researchers has been divided,

244
00:19:22,040 --> 00:19:27,980
and no direct evidence has settled the question. Today's genetic tools cannot settle it either.

245
00:19:28,740 --> 00:19:34,580
Polygenic scores, built mostly from people of European ancestry, lose much of their accuracy

246
00:19:34,580 --> 00:19:40,200
in other populations, and even within one population, they partly pick up family environment.

247
00:19:40,900 --> 00:19:43,300
International comparisons have a further problem.

248
00:19:43,560 --> 00:19:46,720
A widely cited set of national IQ estimates,

249
00:19:46,980 --> 00:19:49,860
compiled by Richard Lynn and Tatu von Hanen,

250
00:19:50,280 --> 00:19:53,180
rests in many countries on one or two small samples

251
00:19:53,180 --> 00:19:55,880
that were never designed to represent the whole country.

252
00:19:56,500 --> 00:19:59,560
In 2010, Wick Hertz and colleagues

253
00:19:59,560 --> 00:20:01,580
reviewed the African data systematically,

254
00:20:01,920 --> 00:20:04,720
with explicit rules for which samples to include.

255
00:20:04,920 --> 00:20:08,240
Their average came out roughly 12 points higher than Lynn's,

256
00:20:10,420 --> 00:20:15,180
They warned that Lynn's figures rested on unsystematic choices of samples,

257
00:20:15,420 --> 00:20:20,760
and that whether the tests measure the same thing in those populations still needed proper study.

258
00:20:21,020 --> 00:20:26,240
Papers built on the dataset are now being re-examined, and at least three have been retracted,

259
00:20:26,420 --> 00:20:32,460
most recently one in a Royal Society journal in 2024 and another in 2025.

260
00:20:33,160 --> 00:20:36,220
Countries really do differ on international school assessments,

261
00:20:36,220 --> 00:20:43,060
But how much of that reflects the same underlying ability, and why the differences exist, are separate questions.

262
00:20:43,320 --> 00:20:48,260
And the Flynn effect is a reminder that national averages move as countries develop.

263
00:20:49,240 --> 00:20:51,620
Two neighboring ideas, briefly.

264
00:20:52,040 --> 00:20:56,440
Howard Gardner's multiple intelligences are hugely popular in education,

265
00:20:56,700 --> 00:21:00,720
but when researchers built measures of the supposedly separate intelligences,

266
00:21:01,040 --> 00:21:03,840
most of them loaded on G and on each other.

267
00:21:04,580 --> 00:21:06,800
Rationality is the more interesting case.

268
00:21:07,020 --> 00:21:12,900
The psychologist Keith Stanovich coined the word dysrationalia for smart people who reason badly.

269
00:21:13,680 --> 00:21:20,180
Resisting a tempting but wrong intuition turns out to be at least partly a different skill from raw reasoning power,

270
00:21:20,420 --> 00:21:24,820
though the two are related, and standard intelligence tests largely miss it.

271
00:21:25,720 --> 00:21:31,980
So if someone tells me a language model has an IQ of 130, what should I ask?

272
00:21:33,840 --> 00:21:39,820
is supposed to measure. Human abilities really do covery, and the general factor that summarizes

273
00:21:39,820 --> 00:21:46,020
them predicts real outcomes, but at least four explanations fit the same data, and the factor's

274
00:21:46,020 --> 00:21:51,640
biological meaning is still open. Then ask whether the test means the same thing for that system

275
00:21:51,640 --> 00:21:56,300
as for the people it was built for, which is the invariance question in a new setting.

276
00:21:56,440 --> 00:22:00,580
And keep in mind how easily the numbers people fight about are misread.

277
00:22:01,260 --> 00:22:04,100
Heritability describes populations, not persons.

278
00:22:04,460 --> 00:22:07,500
It coexists with large environmental effects.

279
00:22:07,780 --> 00:22:11,340
And by itself, it cannot explain differences between groups.

280
00:22:11,660 --> 00:22:17,220
Most of all, notice that everything in this episode has been about how humans compare with other humans.

281
00:22:17,560 --> 00:22:25,560
A test built for schoolchildren tells you little, by itself, about what a crow, an octopus, or a language model has or lacks.

282
00:22:25,960 --> 00:22:28,340
For that, we leave the human case behind.

283
00:22:28,340 --> 00:22:36,960
Next episode, minds and other bodies from a crow that bent a wire into a hook to cells that solve problems without any brain at all.

